(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does VERTICAL transmission mean?","choices":["Between two adults by sexual contact","From a surface to a person","From an animal to a person","Mother to child"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which link does the chain of infection end with?","choices":["A susceptible host","The portal of entry","The reservoir","The mode of transmission"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and susceptibility varies between hosts. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which is an example of vertical transmission?","choices":["Kissing or sexual contact","A mosquito bite","Contaminated food","Mother to child at birth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, transplacental or during vaginal birth. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Why does the marrow RECIPIENT receive drug and radiation therapy beforehand?","choices":["To destroy the donor cells' antigens","To raise the recipient's own stem cell count","To prevent infection during transport","To reduce the risk of rejecting the donor cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — donor and recipient preparation are closely coordinated. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What distinguishes the CD56 dim from the CD56 bright natural killer cell?","choices":["CD56 dim kills virally infected cells","CD56 dim secretes cytokines; CD56 bright kills","Both only kill","Both only secrete cytokines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — two effector functions in subpopulations adapted to their tissue. CD56 bright secretes cytokines to maintain inflammation. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Bacitracin works by which mechanism, and why is it topical only?","choices":["It binds the 30S subunit and causes misreading; it is nephrotoxic","It interferes with bactoprenol, the membrane transporter carrying peptidoglycan monomers to the growing wall, preventing dephosphorylation so no new monomers are inserted; it is too toxic for parenteral use","Amycolatopsis orientalis; a polyene that binds ergosterol in the fungal membrane and forms pores through it, causing leakage of cell contents, which is why it is too toxic for routine systemic use","It inhibits DNA gyrase; it is restricted to urinary tract use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The wall weakens and the bacterium bursts from osmotic lysis. It is produced by Bacillus subtilis and used against Gram-positives. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How many polypeptide chains make up an immunoglobulin, and of what kinds?","choices":["Two — one heavy and one light","Six — three heavy and three light","One continuous chain","Four — two heavy, two light"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, generally Y-shaped, with variable and constant regions. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What distinguishes a sign from a symptom?","choices":["A sign is observed; a symptom is felt by the patient","A sign is felt; a symptom is observed","A sign is early; a symptom is late","A sign is specific; a symptom is non-specific"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and a sign is often measurable by the clinician. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What trend has the last century shown?","choices":["Death rate fell while morbidity stayed high","Both death and morbidity rates fell","Both rose together","Death rate rose while morbidity fell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is the LEAST common in serum and the shortest-lived?","choices":["IgD","IgM","IgA","IgE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it appears mainly against allergens and parasitic worms. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which product category was most often contaminated in the West African hospital study?","choices":["Liquid soap","Bar soap","Alcohol hand rub","Surgical scrub brushes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — 51 of 69 samples. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What does a differential white count actually measure?","choices":["The rate of phagocytosis","The number of each type of white cell","The total white cell count alone","The haemoglobin concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — proportion is the point, not just the total. Whether they are in normal proportion. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What has driven the evolution of multi-drug resistant strains?","choices":["Antimicrobial surface coatings","Shorter hospital stays","Extensive use of antibiotics","Improved hand hygiene"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which toxicity does chloroquine carry?","choices":["Aplastic anaemia","Seizures","Oesophageal ulcerations following oral dosing without water","Cardiovascular effects and electrolyte derangements with dysrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It sits in the same list as the antibacterial toxicities despite being an antiprotozoal. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What do endothelial cells do in response to inflammatory cytokines?","choices":["Begin phagocytosis","Secrete antibody","Upregulate adhesion molecules","Tighten their junctions to seal the vessel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both are needed to let leukocytes leave the vessel. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What effect do crowded indoor environments have on disease?","choices":["They have no measurable effect","They only matter for bacterial disease","They increase morbidity and mortality","They reduce transmission by dilution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — schools, daycare, nursing homes, hotels, cruise ships and hospitals. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which responses should an effective vaccine stimulate?","choices":["Innate immunity only","BOTH antibody and cell-mediated","Antibody-mediated immunity only","Cell-mediated immunity only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it should produce memory B and T cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Why are persistent pathogens on fomites particularly dangerous?","choices":["They look like clean surfaces","They glow under ultraviolet light","They only survive minutes","They are always drug-sensitive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the Major Histocompatibility Complex also known as?","choices":["Immunoglobulin","Human leukocyte antigen","Cluster of differentiation","Antigenic determinant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — MHC and HLA are the same thing. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which features belong to the lytic cycle rather than the lysogenic?","choices":["Viral genes replicated along with the host cell","Specialized transduction, in which all cells carry the same host DNA","Rapid takeover of bacterial metabolism, production of multiple virus copies, and destruction of the host cell","Integration of viral genes as a prophage, replication alongside the host cell, and immunity to reinfection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lytic cycle also produces generalized transduction, in which random pieces of host DNA may be transmitted to other bacteria. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What defines an endemic disease?","choices":["Steady frequency in a particular location","Occasional cases at irregular intervals","Prevalence rising beyond expectation","A global epidemic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, often because the reservoir is present. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Host defense divides into which three categories?","choices":["Humoral, cellular, and chemical","Barriers, fixed innate mechanisms","Skin, blood, and lymph","Bacterial, viral, and parasitic defenses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — physical and chemical barriers, hard-wired innate mechanisms, then adaptive immunity. The adaptive response. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the FOUR things antibodies do once bound to antigen?","choices":["Agglutination","Phagocytosis, degranulation, chemotaxis and lysis","Fever, inflammation, swelling and pain","Recognition, processing, presentation and activation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the four functions of the binding sites. Opsonisation, complement fixation and neutralisation. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which four problems with antimicrobial therapy go together?","choices":["Bactericidal failure, bacteriostatic failure, poor absorption and rapid clearance","Misuse, overuse, incomplete regimens and leftover prescriptions","Cost, availability, storage and administration route","Superinfection, direct toxicity, hypersensitive reactions and resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those four are the recognised problems. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the lacrimal apparatus contribute beyond washing the eye?","choices":["Defensins from Paneth cells","Lysozyme, a chemical defence","Complement","Interferon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — so tears are a physical AND a chemical barrier. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why must a room be vacant before using ozone or high-concentration hydrogen peroxide?","choices":["To let the surfaces dry first","To avoid disturbing the patient's sleep","To allow the temperature to drop","To reduce harm to humans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which pattern describes several microbes growing at one site simultaneously?","choices":["Mixed infection","Secondary infection","Focal infection","Systemic infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the National Antimicrobial Resistance Monitoring System for Enteric Bacteria monitor, and through which agencies?","choices":["Only retail meats, through the Food and Drug Administration","Antibiotic prescribing rates in outpatient clinics","Antimicrobial resistance of enteric bacteria from humans (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention), retail meats (Food and Drug Administration) and food animals (United States Department of Agriculture), with state and local health departments","Only hospital-acquired infections, through the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention alone, with data drawn from inpatient isolates and reported annually to state health departments for regional comparison"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is an interagency partnership spanning all three sources. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What distinguishes SPECIFIC immunity from innate immunity?","choices":["It requires no lymphocytes","It is adaptive","It is present from birth","It works only against bacteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Innate immunity is what we are born with; specific immunity has to be acquired. Acquired through contact with infectious agents. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"The resistance mechanisms are grouped under a heading describing what problem?","choices":["The organism grows faster than the drug can act","The host clears the drug before it can work","The antimicrobial does not get to the active site — by decreased permeability, decreased transport, or increased efflux","The drug is inherently bacteriostatic rather than bactericidal, so it slows the organism without ever clearing it from the host"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those three are grouped together as failures of access, before the separate categories of inactivation, target modification and bypass. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What health implications follow from genetic mutations in HUMAN hosts?","choices":["Changes to surface antigens that evade vaccination","Acquisition of resistance to antibiotics and other control methods, the ability to produce toxins, and other virulence factors enhancing pathogenicity","Chemical or radiation exposure transforming normal cells to cancer cells, oncogenic viruses causing the same transformation, and viruses affecting immune cells","Loss of resident flora and overgrowth of pathogens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Human immunodeficiency virus with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, and autoimmune disorders, are the examples given. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Can a zoonotic infection be eliminated?","choices":["It requires only vector control","It is eliminated by vaccinating the human population alone","It cannot be eliminated without eradicating the animal reservoir","It is eliminated once human cases stop"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Viroids differ from prions in which way?","choices":["Viroids are short pieces of RNA; prions contain no nucleic acid at all","Viroids are misfolded proteins; prions are nucleic acid","Viroids cause spongiform encephalopathies; prions infect plants","Viroids have a protein coat; prions do not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Viroids have so far been identified only in plants. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is a myeloid dendritic cell's job?","choices":["Kill virus-infected cells directly","Produce antibody","Form the ciliary escalator","Patrol tissues, take up antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the bridge to an adaptive response. Transport it to the nearest lymph node for T cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which body publishes the Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report?","choices":["The National Institutes of Health","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","The World Health Organization in Geneva","The Food and Drug Administration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; the World Health Organization is the international counterpart. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Streptogramins are distinctive among the protein synthesis inhibitors because they do what?","choices":["Bind the 30S subunit and cause codon misreading","Interfere with transfer RNA attachment","Use two different components that work together to bind the 50S subunit and inhibit protein translation","Inhibit folic acid synthesis because their structure closely resembles that of para-aminobenzoic acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are produced by Streptomyces species and work against Gram-positives. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which practice was named as a risk factor for contaminating antiseptics?","choices":["Refrigerating the product","Using single-use sachets","Labelling containers with dates","Topping up containers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — with recycled bottles and inconsistent preparation. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a genetic mechanism of developing antibiotic resistance?","choices":["Plasmid swapping during conjugation","Spontaneous reversion of the bacterial chromosome to a wild type state","Spontaneous loss of the resistance plasmid during binary fission","Random genetic mutation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The listed mechanisms are random mutation, plasmid swapping during conjugation, movement of transposons, transduction by bacteriophages, transformation, and binary fission sharing any of the above. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which is given as an example of a systemic infection?","choices":["Antibiotic-associated colitis","Measles","A boil","A wart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with chicken pox, anthrax and rabies. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does NADPH oxidase produce in the respiratory burst, and what does that achieve?","choices":["Nitric oxide, which relaxes the vessel","Lysozyme, which digests peptidoglycan","Superoxide, which raises the pH","Hydrogen peroxide, which is immediately degraded by catalase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the pH shift is what enables digestion. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Prions are described as which of the following?","choices":["Protein coats enclosing a nucleic acid genome","Misfolded proteins containing no nucleic acid, causing spongiform encephalopathies","Tiny obligate intracellular bacteria that lack a cell wall, causing spongiform encephalopathies","Short pieces of RNA with no protein coat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Examples given are scrapie in sheep and goats, bovine spongiform encephalopathy, and Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease in humans. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How is the therapeutic index expressed?","choices":["Maximum tolerated dose over minimum inhibitory concentration, equivalently median toxic dose (TD50) over median effective dose (ED50)","Renal clearance plus hepatic clearance, divided by total clearance","Minimal inhibitory concentration divided by minimal bactericidal concentration, equivalently the trough over the peak plasma level","Zone of inhibition divided by disk concentration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both forms are given, and it is a calculation used to assess the safety and efficacy of a drug. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which molecule is by far the most important in the complement cascade?","choices":["C5","C9","C3","C1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the pathways differ mainly in how they get to it. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Polymyxin B acts how, and how is it typically used?","choices":["It inhibits squalene epoxidase; applied to skin","It disrupts both the outer membrane of Gram-negative cells and the inner membrane by attaching to lipid components; used topically, combined with bacitracin and neomycin in over-the-counter preparations","Inhibiting bacterial RNA polymerase and so preventing transcription; used chiefly against tuberculosis and other mycobacterial infections, always in combination with a second agent to limit resistance","It blocks beta-glucan synthesis; given intravenously"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is produced by Paenibacillus polymyxa. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Influenza virus binds host cells using which molecule, and to which receptor?","choices":["Panton-Valentine leucocidin, binding receptors on neutrophils","Lipid A, binding pattern recognition receptors on white blood cells","Hemagglutinin protein, binding sialic acid receptors on host cells","Type I fimbriae, binding mannose receptors on host cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is the specific molecular interaction between a pathogen surface protein and a host cell receptor. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is adsorption, in phage replication?","choices":["Binding of the virus to a specific molecule on the host cell","The departure of completed virions to go on and infect other cells","The completion of viral formation","The entry of the genome into the host cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Specificity at this first step is what determines host range. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is a passive carrier?","choices":["An animal that transmits disease","Someone recovered but still shedding","Someone such as medical or dental personnel","Someone with an obvious active infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — carrying the organism without being infected by it. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which mnemonic gives the leukocytes from HIGHEST to lowest percentage?","choices":["Basophils eat many new lymphocytes","Monocytes never eat basophil lymphocytes","Never let monkeys eat bananas","Leukocytes make everyone nervous, briefly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How do natural killer cells and macrophages cooperate?","choices":["Macrophages recruit and activate natural killer cells","Natural killer cells recruit macrophages, which return interleukin 12","They compete for the same targets","They do not interact"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a reciprocal loop at the infection site. Those return interferon gamma, enhancing macrophage phagocytosis. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which antibodies are produced in the PRIMARY response?","choices":["IgG only, in very large titre","IgA only","IgE only","IgM and IgG"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, along with memory B cells specific for that antigen. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Chloramphenicol and erythromycin both bind the 50S ribosomal subunit. What distinguishes their actions?","choices":["Chloramphenicol works against Gram-positives only; erythromycin is broad spectrum","Chloramphenicol is synthetic; erythromycin is produced by Streptomyces venezuelae","Chloramphenicol changes the shape of the 30S ribosomal subunit; erythromycin blocks the attachment of transfer RNA to the ribosome entirely","Chloramphenicol inhibits peptide bond formation; erythromycin prevents amino acid translocation from the transfer RNA to the ribosomal binding site"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chloramphenicol is broad spectrum, erythromycin works against Gram-positives. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which practice did Semmelweis introduce in the 1840s?","choices":["Handwashing with carbolic acid","Sterile surgical gloves","Antibiotic prophylaxis","Handwashing with chlorine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What does a small infectious dose imply about an organism?","choices":["Reduced transmissibility","Greater virulence","Lower virulence","A longer incubation period"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and therefore a greater degree of pathogenicity. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A culture of roughly 4,865,321 organisms is exposed to a control method with a decimal reduction time of 10 minutes. What does the constant death rate imply about clearing it?","choices":["Nothing can be predicted, because death rate varies with population size","Each successive 10-minute interval removes 90% of whatever remains, so the count falls by an order of magnitude each time","The population reaches zero after exactly two intervals","Each successive 10-minute interval removes a fixed NUMBER of organisms, so the count falls in equal steps each time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. From about 4.9 million, successive intervals give roughly 486,000, then 48,600, then 4,860 — which is why sterilisation is defined by probability rather than by a moment when the last organism dies. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which four properties should a hospital surface coating have?","choices":["Porous, flexible, warm and light","Nontoxic, cost effective, stable and durable","Colourful, cheap, disposable and soft","Reflective, rigid, heavy and opaque"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and commercially available. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Responses that stimulate B cells are called what?","choices":["Antibody-mediated immunity","Cell-mediated immunity","Innate immunity","Species immunity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — AMI, or HMI in older textbooks. Or humoral-mediated immunity. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Crosslinking of peptidoglycan is catalyzed by transpeptidases. Why does that matter clinically?","choices":["Because transpeptidases produce the endospore coat","Because transpeptidases are the receptor for bacterial conjugation","Because transpeptidases determine whether a cell retains crystal violet","Because transpeptidases are the target of beta lactam antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transpeptidases are the beta-lactam target, which is what connects wall synthesis to antibiotic action. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why does a Gram-negative organism appear red at the end of the stain?","choices":["It loses crystal violet during decolorization and takes up the safranin counterstain","It never binds the crystal violet stain in the first place during the procedure","Its teichoic acid binds safranin preferentially","Its thick peptidoglycan sheath traps safranin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Retention or loss of crystal violet is what the whole technique turns on. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What property should an antimicrobial chemotherapeutic agent have, and what does that mean?","choices":["Broad spectrum — active against every class of organism","Bactericidal action — killing rather than inhibiting","Natural origin — a substance produced by one microorganism against another","Selective toxicity — working against the microbial agent without harming the host"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin, from the Penicillium mold and active against Gram-positive bacteria, is the example given. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How does interferon protect uninfected cells?","choices":["It forms pores in their membranes","It presents viral antigen to T cells","It induces antiviral proteins in them","It coats them in sialic acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it prepares the neighbours. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 59"}]};
